WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.325
Conditional aggregation lets one query calculate
several metrics from the same set

00:00:04.338 --> 00:00:08.488
of source rows The main idea is that a condition
controls which

00:00:08.512 --> 00:00:13.350
rows contribute to each aggregate For example from
the same admissions table

00:00:13.375 --> 00:00:16.988
we might calculate one count for pneumonia and
another count for fractures

00:00:17.875 --> 00:00:22.425
There are two common approaches in this module CASE
is highly flexible

00:00:22.438 --> 00:00:26.938
and works across many SQL systems We can place a
CASE expression

00:00:26.962 --> 00:00:30.800
inside SUM COUNT or another aggregate and map
matching rows to a

00:00:30.812 --> 00:00:36.612
useful value PostgreSQL also supports FILTER FILTER
places

00:00:36.638 --> 00:00:41.188
a WHERE-like condition directly on an aggregate
function It is often very

00:00:41.212 --> 00:00:44.362
readable when we want several independent counts or
sums in the same

00:00:44.375 --> 00:00:49.812
SELECT list Both approaches are useful CASE is more
portable and can

00:00:49.838 --> 00:00:56.300
handle complex mappings FILTER is concise and
expressive in PostgreSQL The

00:00:56.312 --> 00:01:00.875
important concept is not the exact syntax It is that
one grouped

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:04.000
row can contain several measures that each look at a
different subset

00:01:04.025 --> 00:01:08.550
of the same underlying rows This is extremely useful
for dashboards and

00:01:08.575 --> 00:01:12.512
feature tables because it avoids writing separate
queries for every category or

00:01:12.525 --> 00:01:13.088
condition
